# tier: original-diagnostic
# oracle: independent-derivation
# scenario: diag-ctrl-psg-pass
# profile: intv-2609-ntsc-family-p1
# program: fixtures/FX-diag-ctrl-psg-1/diag-ctrl-psg-1.listing.txt (ivs-diag-ctrl-psg-1), entered through
#   fixtures/FX-boot-stub/boot-stub.listing.txt (contract 1: J $5000 with R6 = 0340)
#
# Predicate of ivs-diag-ctrl-psg-1 (D-24, V-06, V-07, A-05): the guest state at the stop and the PSG
# write list, derived by hand from the controller literal (data/input/intv-2609-ntsc-family-p1.literal.txt),
# the register-address literal (data/psg/intv-2609-ntsc-family-p1.regmap.literal.txt) and the printed
# CP-1610 instruction tables, in plan 05-01 (docs/input/diag-ctrl-psg-1-derivation.md, commit 14a1904),
# BEFORE the program, the scenario or any PSG code existed; it moves here with the scenario (A-05). It was
# not generated by, read back from or edited to match the core. The author of this derivation also wrote
# the core, the literals and the program, so all four can share one misreading of a source; the claims
# that rest on this file (CL-0014, CL-0015) stay provisional for that reason. The controller bytes rest on
# two community pages that are likely one lineage (C16), the register map on one community page (C70),
# the tone-A silence and the write-effect instant on project conventions (C77, C78).
# Excluded on purpose: machine time (the halt near tick 16,700 and the 81-cycle poll loop are regression
# information; the branch counts are policy C29 and the reset entry an inference), R7 and the flags at the
# stop (C40, C45, C56), all registers (the scenario asserts memory, PSG registers and the write list only),
# the stack word [0340] (C68), the ticks of the PSG write records (printed in the report, never asserted),
# the upper byte of a port read (C64: the guest masks every port read with ANDI #$00FF, so the RAM tables
# do not depend on it), and the read-back of the envelope shape register (C75).
# Policy tags: lines tagged `# policy: C64` assert a full 16-bit read of a PSG register whose upper byte
# is the default $00 (device kind psg8 fill, D-04, discrepancy C64); the line tagged `# policy: C75`
# asserts the shape register read-back, which the default read mask $0000 gives as 0 (discrepancy C75).
#
# derivation: program. Eleven PSG programming stores (MVII #value,R0; MVO R0,$addr) in the order
#   $01F0=0023, $01F4=0001, $01F8=003E, $01FB=000F, $01F1=0080, $01F5=0000, $01FC=0008, $01F9=0010,
#   $01F3=0064, $01F7=0000, $01FA=000A. The register-address literal gives the register numbers 0, 4, 8,
#   11, 1, 5, 12, 9, 3, 7, 10 (the low nibble of the address). Channel A period is $123 (coarse nibble 1,
#   fine byte $23). The mixer value 003E = 0011 1110 enables tone A only; bits 6 and 7 are clear so both
#   ports stay inputs (a set bit makes the port an output and a read would return the latch, so the poll
#   below would never see a change).
# derivation: poll. R1 = R2 = 0100 (previous port values, which no masked read can equal), R4 = 0210 and
#   R5 = 0220 (store pointers, auto-incremented by MVO@), R3 = 8 (port 0 values still to store). The loop
#   reads $01FE, keeps the low byte, and stores it at [R4] when it differs from R1; the same for $01FF
#   with R2 and R5; it ends when eight port 0 values are stored.
# derivation: bytes read = FF XOR the OR of the cleared masks of the pressed controls (literal masks:
#   KEY_1 81, KEY_9 24, KEY_5 42, DISC_N 04, DISC_E 02, SIDE_TOP A0, SIDE_LOWER_LEFT 60).
#   port 0: start (none) FF; 4000 KEY_1 -> 81 -> 7E; 4800 none -> FF; 5600 KEY_1+KEY_9 -> 81 OR 24 = A5
#   -> 5A; 6400 none -> FF; 7200 DISC_N -> 04 -> FB; 8000 DISC_N+SIDE_TOP -> 04 OR A0 = A4 -> 5B; 8800
#   KEY_5+SIDE_TOP -> 42 OR A0 = E2 -> 1D (a guest-ambiguous pair of class key-side).
#   port 1: start FF; 4800 DISC_E -> 02 -> FD; 5600 SIDE_LOWER_LEFT -> 60 -> 9F; 6400 none -> FF.
#   Port 0 stored at [0210..0217]: FF 7E FF 5A FF FB 5B 1D (eight values, so the loop ends); port 1 at
#   [0220..0223]: FF FD 9F FF; the other words of the tables stay 0. Events are at least 600 ticks apart,
#   far above one poll loop (324 ticks), so every state is polled at least once before the next event.
# derivation: end. A counted delay of 120 iterations (about 7,200 ticks) follows the last store, then
#   MVII #$03C5,R0; MVO R0,$020F writes the end marker last, then HLT and three TSTR R0 followers.
# derivation: write list. The PSG register-write log of the run holds exactly the eleven writes above,
#   in that order, with source cpu-write; the address field is the low nibble of the bus address.
# derivation: read-back. Each programmed register reads back through the profile read mask (C75 for the
#   amplitude and shape registers): $01F0 0023, $01F4 0001 (coarse nibble), $01F8 003E, $01FB 000F,
#   $01F1 0080, $01F5 0000, $01FC 0008, $01F9 0010, $01F3 0064, $01F7 0000; the shape register $01FA was
#   written 000A and reads 0000 (C75).
# derivation: chip-side consequences (properties, tested in tests/run/run_diag_ctrl.c, not asserted
#   here). Channel B has its tone bit and noise bit disabled, so its level is its amplitude 8 from the
#   write at $01FC on; channel C was never written, so it stays 0. Tone A stays silent for the whole run:
#   its counter reloaded at the first step with the then-zero period (4096 steps = 65,536 ticks), so the
#   period written later takes effect only after about tick 65,536 (C77, policy counter_model).
#
# Body: one assert per clause in the runner's scenario grammar, hex upper case, exact compare, evaluated
# at the stop, in the order of data/scenarios/diag-ctrl-psg-pass.ivss (golden-expect-drift).
assert halted == yes
assert mem 020F == 03C5
assert mem 0210 == 00FF
assert mem 0211 == 007E
assert mem 0212 == 00FF
assert mem 0213 == 005A
assert mem 0214 == 00FF
assert mem 0215 == 00FB
assert mem 0216 == 005B
assert mem 0217 == 001D
assert mem 0220 == 00FF
assert mem 0221 == 00FD
assert mem 0222 == 009F
assert mem 0223 == 00FF
assert mem 0224 == 0000
assert mem 0225 == 0000
assert mem 0226 == 0000
assert mem 0227 == 0000
# policy: C64
assert mem 01F0 == 0023
# policy: C64
assert mem 01F4 == 0001
# policy: C64
assert mem 01F8 == 003E
# policy: C64
assert mem 01FB == 000F
# policy: C64
assert mem 01F1 == 0080
# policy: C64
assert mem 01F5 == 0000
# policy: C64
assert mem 01FC == 0008
# policy: C64
assert mem 01F9 == 0010
# policy: C64
assert mem 01F3 == 0064
# policy: C64
assert mem 01F7 == 0000
# policy: C75
assert mem 01FA == 0000
assert psglog count == 11
assert psglog 0 addr=0 value=0023 source=cpu-write
assert psglog 1 addr=4 value=0001 source=cpu-write
assert psglog 2 addr=8 value=003E source=cpu-write
assert psglog 3 addr=11 value=000F source=cpu-write
assert psglog 4 addr=1 value=0080 source=cpu-write
assert psglog 5 addr=5 value=0000 source=cpu-write
assert psglog 6 addr=12 value=0008 source=cpu-write
assert psglog 7 addr=9 value=0010 source=cpu-write
assert psglog 8 addr=3 value=0064 source=cpu-write
assert psglog 9 addr=7 value=0000 source=cpu-write
assert psglog 10 addr=10 value=000A source=cpu-write
